rcise  for any value of n that we want and it  is quite instructive to do it to let you  play with this I have decided to try  something new in this video I have  written a Jupiter notebook implementing  some Python code to describe the wave  function so check it out in the  description where there is a link to my  github if you think this is a good  and you would like to be able to play  more with code in future videos please  let me know in the comments what I have  here are two examples of the output of  the code so the first one here  corresponds to the N equals 10 energy  level this is an even n so it is sine  wave function and you can see that  indeed it goes to 0 at the origin the  one in orange corresponds to N equals  100 in this case there are many pigs  they are very close together so we can  draw a horizontal line here which tells  us that the absolute value squared of  the wave function is roughly constant to  some extent the result for very large n  can be understood by making a comparison  with what happens in classical physics  to do that we can see the classical  particle moving to the left inside the  box with a fixed kinetic energy 1/2 MV  squared the classical particle will move  towards the left at constant speed then  it will hit the wall reverse direction  and move towards the right at the same  speed and it will hit the other wall and  go back so we'll have this back and  forth motion inside the box in classical  physics if we know the position and the  velocity of a particle at a given time  the Newton's laws tell us where it will  be at any time in the future but for the  sake of argument let's imagine that all  we know is that the particle is moving  back and forth at constant speed but we  actually don't know what it started in  this case we can define a probability  density P of X which is telling us where  the particle could be because the  particle is moving at constant speed  then that means it is equally likely to  be found anywhere inside the box that  means that the probability is constant  and it is equal to 1 over a if we want  to normalize it with respect to the size  of the box if we compare this constant  probability with the constant  probability that we obtain for very  large energy levels in the quantum case  we then see that there is some sort of  analogy between the two and that's why  some people call the quantum case for  very large n the classical limit I  wouldn't want you to take this analogy  too seriously because in fact the  quantum and the classical cases are  fundamentally different when we know all  the information there is to know about  the classical case we have no  probabilities we know precisely where  the particle will be at any point in the  future the quantum case is fundamentally  different even when we know everything  there is  know about the quantum system that is we  know the wave function we still have a  probabilistic interpretation and we  cannot say for sure where a particle is  this becomes very obvious when we make a  measurement of the position of a quantum  particle and if you want to learn more  about that and to see how weird quantum  mechanics can become then check out the  video in the description so what have we  learned with the infinite square well  potential this iconic potential has  taught us about some peculiarities of  quantum theory most importantly the  quantization of energy levels there are  many other weird consequences of quantum  theory in the description I have  included links to a few videos where I  talk about for example how exciting  things can get when you measure what a  particle is or you can learn about  something as weird as quantum tunneling  if you liked the video and would like  some suggestions for future videos  please subscribe
 

